README-GREP -- six ways to search a file, and which one to takeIf you are copying part of this collection onto your own media you want ONEof these.  This file is here so you do not have to install six and try them.Short answer: take `grep'.  It is GNU grep 2.0, it needs no Microware module,and it is the only one of the six that answered every form asked of it.  Take`bm' as well only if you are searching very large files for a fixed string andhave `cio'.THE SIX FILES-------------  file      bytes  needs  module   what it is                   cio  ---------------------------------------------------------------------  grep      72364  no     grep     GNU grep 2.0  ggrep     53124  yes    ggrep    GNU grep, from the sh_utils collection  fgrep     19960  yes    fgrep    fixed-string search, EFFO forum disk 14  bm         6834  yes    bm       Boyer-Moore fast search  gep        3654  yes    gep      global expression parser -- takes its                                   patterns from a FILE  lgrep      2446  yes    lgrep    "same as grep -l" by its own bannerWHAT EACH ONE DID, RUN ON THE SAME FILE---------------------------------------Measured 2026-08-31, all six against /dd/SYS/motd looking for `os9exec':  grep -c os9exec <file>        1  ggrep -c os9exec <file>       1  fgrep os9exec <file>          printed the matching line  bm os9exec <file>             printed the matching line  gep -e=<exprfile> <file>      printed the matching line  lgrep os9exec <file>          NOTHING, and exit status 0  grep -l os9exec <f1> <f2>     printed both filenames`lgrep' DOES NOT WORK HERE.  Its own banner says "same as 'grep -l', butprints filenames without comments", and `grep -l' does exactly that on thesame two files -- so the job is done, just not by lgrep.  It printed nothingand exited 0 both as a bare program and from a shell with PATH set to/dd/CMDS.  DOC/INDEX described it as "line grep", which is not what its ownbanner says either; both are corrected there now.`gep' IS NOT A DROP-IN.  Its `-e' takes the PATH OF A FILE containing theexpressions, not an expression: `gep -e=os9exec <file>' answers "can't openexpression path 'os9exec'".  Put the patterns in a file, one per line, and`gep -e=/dd/tmp/patterns <file>' works.  That file of patterns is the wholepoint of it -- it is for running many searches at once, which none of theothers do.TAKE THIS ONE IF----------------  grep     You want one that works and asks for nothing.  GNU grep 2.0:           regular expressions, -c, -l, -i, -v and the rest.  It is the           largest at 71K, and it is the only one here that needs no           Microware runtime module.  ggrep    You already have cio and want GNU behaviour in 20K less.  It came           from the sh_utils collection.  Nothing was found that it does           differently from `grep'.  fgrep    You want fixed strings only -- no regular expressions -- in a           fifth of grep's space.  No source survived in its archive; it is           the archive's own prebuilt binary.  bm       You are searching large files for a fixed string and want it           fast.  Boyer-Moore, and the smallest of the ones that work at           6.6K.  gep      You have MANY patterns to apply at once and want them kept in a           file rather than on the command line.  Nothing else here does           that.  lgrep    Nothing: it produced no output in any form tried.  `grep -l'           does its job.CHOOSING--------  You want one search tool and no surprises          grep  You have no cio and do not want to need one        grep -- it is the only                                                     one of the six  You want fixed strings in a small binary           fgrep, or bm for speed  You have a file full of patterns to apply          gep  You want the filenames rather than the lines       grep -l  If you take exactly one file and nothing else: `grep'.